{"step_index":2230,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T15:10:06Z","input_tokens":3078,"cache_read_tokens":126965,"output_tokens":1839,"content":"### How the Seed Image and Cozo Store Image Are Stored and Accessed\n\nTo keep the repository clean (without bloating Git with multi-gigabyte binary files) while making the assets instantly accessible to the provisioning script:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Seed Image: Host Storage & Distribution\n\n* **Authoritative Location on Host**:\n  `/var/lib/incus/storage-pools/3tched/3tched-seed.img1`\n  This is the shared, immutable read-only seed. It stays permanently mounted or loop-bound on the host. Every container references this exact path.\n* **Storage & Distribution Across Hosts**:\n  * Git stores only the **provenance manifest & checksum** (`assets/seed-manifest.json` with the SHA-256 hash and Btrfs generation ID).\n  * For cold provisioning on new servers, the compressed sparse image (`3tched-seed.img1.zst`) is pulled from the release artifact store or transferred via `btrfs send/receive`.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Cozo Store Image: The Instant Golden Template\n\nBecause the per-container 20 GiB image is 99% empty space (sparse):\n* **The Master Blank Template**:\n  We maintain one pre-formatted golden template at:\n  `/var/lib/op-dbus/identity-cozo/templates/cozo-device-template-20g.img`\n  *(Pre-partitioned with `p1` Btrfs and `p2` ext4).*\n* **Instant CoW Clones (Zero Disk Space, Zero Delay)**:\n  When provisioning a new container (e.g. `id-1`), the script does not format from scratch. It runs:\n  ```bash\n  cp --reflink=always /var/lib/op-dbus/identity-cozo/templates/cozo-device-template-20g.img \\\n                      /var/lib/op-dbus/identity-cozo/devices/id-1.img\n  ```\n  On Btrfs, `cp --reflink` is **instantaneous (0.01s) and consumes 0 extra bytes of disk space**.\n* **Automatic UUID Randomization**:\n  The script immediately stamps fresh, random UUIDs onto the clone (`btrfstune -U` and `tune2fs -U`) so zero collisions can ever occur.\n\n---\n\n### Exactly What I Propose to Do to Button Everything Up\n\nIn `/srv/git/3tched-container`:\n\n1. **Step 1: Create the Golden Template Directory**:\n   Set up `/var/lib/op-dbus/identity-cozo/templates/` and generate the single master pre-formatted template using `make-cozo-device.sh`.\n2. **Step 2: Button Up `provision-identity.sh`**:\n   Refine the script so it:\n   * Takes the instance name (e.g. `id-1`) and the detached cryptographic ID card.\n   * Clones the pre-formatted device via `cp --reflink=always`.\n   * Randomizes the partition UUIDs.\n   * Creates the Incus container bound to the shared seed.\n   * Runs `btrfs device add /dev/cozo-disk1 /` to sprout the leaf.\n   * Registers the pair in Cozo.\n3. **Step 3: Add `assets/manifest.json`**:\n   Commit the exact paths, SHA-256 hashes, and configuration schema into `/srv/git/3tched-container`.\n\n---\n\n### Confirmation Request\n\nThis will generate the master template in `/var/lib/op-dbus/identity-cozo/templates/` and button up `provision-identity.sh` in `/srv/git/3tched-container`. It will not touch any running containers or active services.\n\n**Do you confirm proceeding with these steps to button up the provisioning pipeline?**","thinking":"Handler: CONFIRM. The user needs guidance on storing the seed PA image and OSZO store image for the container provisioning process. A potential approach involves using a container registry (private or public) or a dedicated artifact repository to ensure the images are accessible to the provisioning script. Before providing specific recommendations, confirmation is needed on the preferred method of image storage (container registry, artifact repository, or other).\n\n\nThe seed image can be made accessible to provisioning through a few potential methods: local access via a defined storage path, and external access using a designated artifact repository. Storing large images directly in Git is not feasible. The authoritative on-disk location is either the storage pool or the \"golden\" path, both of which are accessible. The next step involves confirming the preferred access method and the specific provisioning script's capabilities.\n\n\n"}
