ver or new region), it streams the image directly from the **storage bucket next to Qdrant**, verifies the SHA-256 hash, and binds it to Incus.\n3. **One Single Infrastructure Plane**:\n   All core shared assets live side-by-side in the backend tier: Qdrant for vector embeddings, the storage bucket for base images, and EMQX for messages.\n\n---\n\n### Exactly What I Propose to Wire\n\n1. **Define the Asset Manifest**:\n   In `/srv/git/3tched-container/assets/manifest.json`, define the bucket artifact references, object keys, and verification checksums for the seed and Cozo device template.\n2. **Add Bucket Hydration to Provisioning**:\n   In `/srv/git/3tched-container/scripts/provision-identity.sh`, add a check that verifies the local image or fetches it from the bucket endpoint if not present.\n3. **Stage the Bucket Storage Location**:\n   Prepare the storage directory/bucket next to Qdrant on the host.\n\n---\n\n### Confirmation Request\n\nThis will add `manifest.json` and bucket download logic into `/srv/git/3tched-container`. It will not touch running containers or active services.\n\n**Do you confirm configuring the storage bucket integration in `/srv/git/3tched-container`?**","thinking":"The user's statement indicates they need a shared component accessible for provisioning, suggesting a storage bucket next to the quadrant. Confirming this placement is correct and that it meets provisioning requirements is essential before moving forward. Before making any changes, the plan is to first identify the current location of the component and confirm with the user this move is correct.\n\n\nThe proposed architecture places a storage bucket, co-located with the existing infrastructure service, to house immutable image assets and templates. Provisioning scripts within a Git repository can then dynamically retrieve these assets, eliminating reliance on hardcoded paths and ensuring portability. This design enables a modular, Git-based provisioning system where image asse